# Warp operating rules

- `.warp/beam.json` is the source of truth. Update it only through `scripts/beam.py` or `scripts/scan.py`.
- Do not dispatch unless `runState` is `running`.
- Do not start a ticket until every dep is merged and every blocking upstream gate is green.
- Do not run two active tickets whose lock paths overlap.
- The orchestrator is the only merger. It dispatches, merges, and tracks, and it writes no ticket product code. Shuttles and Reed do not merge and do not push to the base branch.
- The only concurrency cap is `maxAgents`. A project may set 18. Do not hardcode a cap.
- One ticket, one branch. `subagentVm` defaults to false. `runner: local` forces it false and forces `launch` to `worktree`, skips the cloud-environment start check, and logs one note. Start, `/warp-version`, and status print the effective values. `maxLocalSubagents` defaults to 18. `memoryCheck` defaults to false, so only that cap applies until `true` lets `free -g` lower it. On a cloud runner, `true` asks for a dedicated VM. The prompt says: run in your own cloud environment on a dedicated VM with its own clone and branch, not a git worktree on this machine. Start one subagent per ticket, in parallel up to `maxAgents`. The same hostname falls back to a worktree on this machine, capped by `maxLocalSubagents`, with one warning per run. `/in-cloud` in the Agents Window is the manual fallback, one ticket per invocation. Cloud subagents use the MCP servers at cursor.com/agents, not this session. Start one subagent per ticket in its own worktree, in parallel up to `maxAgents`. `checkout.py launch` fetches origin and runs `git worktree add <worktreeRoot>/<id> -b warp/<id>-<jira> origin/main`. The prompt names the ticket, Jira key, locks, acceptance, branch, and absolute worktree path. The subagent works only inside that path, commits, pushes, and opens the pull request. It never merges, never touches the parent checkout, and never calls Jira or Slack. It writes `.warp/tickets/<id>/state.json` and `log.jsonl` into the parent checkout by absolute path. `checkout.py verify` raises `lock-escape` when the parent is dirty outside `.warp/` or the diff leaves the locks. Merge or park runs `git worktree remove` and `git worktree prune`. A failed result raises `worker-died`. `/warp-resume` reuses the surviving worktree and branch. Optional `launch: agent` prints an IMPLEMENT prompt for one new Agent. This plugin does not call a Cloud Agents API. Clone main so `.cursor` rules load, then create the branch. A beam file does not have to exist on the branch before that Agent starts. Never two tickets in one worktree. Never two workers on one ticket. The beam stores the agent id or the worktree path.
- Ready means every dependency and every `after` id is merged on the base branch, and no lock path overlaps a lock held by an in-flight ticket. Overlap is the same path or a parent folder. Use the ticket's full lock list. An open or green pull request is not merged.
- Dispatch at start and after every merge, failure, and freed slot. Starred and priority first, then lowest rank, until `maxAgents` are in flight. Do not hold a slot for a ticket that is not ready. Do not wait for a whole dependency level.
- Opening a pull request does not finish the ticket. The slot stays occupied until the GitHub or Bitbucket check rollup is green. Bugbot runs on the pull request after push, not inside the VM. Locks stay until merge or park. `checkCommand` empty means Bugbot and CI as configured. `make ci` is kept as the full check command. A red result is stored on `pr.check` and the ticket is sent back with the log (`send-back <id> fix` and a `start` line). The third red parks. The name alone is not a red check. The gate stays while that check is red. `appendOnlyPaths` is empty by default. A conflict there keeps both sides, every added line. Any other conflict is sent back. Do not hand-merge it. `mergeQueue` defaults to false. When it is true, or GitHub reports a merge queue, enqueue the pull request. A direct merge that branch protection rejects is not marked merged. `state_commit.py commit` commits the beam, journal, STATUS, and BOARD and strips tokens, cost, API keys, and webhook URLs. `.warp/config.yaml` stays gitignored. On a cloud runner, `/warp-start` refuses when `transitionJiraIssue`, `addOrEditJiraIssueComment`, or `slack_send_message` is missing from the MCP allow list. `--force` starts anyway. A local runner does not block.
- The merge queue is serial: starred first, then lowest rank. Rebase, run the check, merge, delete the branch, dispatch. Sizes outside `autoMergeSizes` still wait for `/warp-proceed` or `warp:proceed`. The third red check parks the ticket, releases its locks, and leaves the branch and pull request open. A red base branch stops merging and a fix is dispatched ahead of every rank. Restart classifies merged, in flight, and pending from git.
- On start or resume, rebuild from git: merged on the base branch, in flight when a branch or pull request is open, otherwise pending. Done means every ticket is merged or explicitly parked, and the base branch is green.
- There is no per-person cap. The only concurrency cap is `maxAgents`.
- A size in `autoMergeSizes` auto-merges after Bugbot and CI are green, including L and XL when they are listed. L and XL are not special. The default list is S, M, so those wait for a provider approval or `warp:proceed <id>` unless you add them. QA Ready is not set before Bugbot is clean.
- The git provider is `gitProvider` in `.warp/config.yaml` (`auto`, `github`, or `bitbucket`). `pushMerge: false` means local branches and a local merge, no push and no pull request.
- Coding model comes from `.warp/config.yaml`. Default `claude-sonnet-5-5-high` (Claude Sonnet 5.5 High). The slug must match the Cursor model picker.
- Jira, GitHub, Bitbucket, Slack, and Teams go through Cursor's connected MCP servers, or `gh` for GitHub when `ghCli` is true. If none is usable, fall back to local-only.
- When `beam.py set` prints `jira: MUST DO`, do every action in `.warp/jira-todo.json` in that turn, then `jira_sync.py record` and `record-comment`. A later failure goes to `.warp/outbox.md` and Herald and does not stop that ticket. The first claimed ticket of a run is different: if Jira says the issue was not found, or no real issue key can be resolved, release the claim back to `queued` and stop the run. `jiraTransition: false` does not stop the run.
- The listener is a Subagent of the parent. Start one subagent per ticket in its own worktree, in parallel up to `maxAgents`. The listener shares the parent's session and checkout. It is not a separate Agent. Exactly one listener while the loop is running. It loops: poll Slack and Teams, post acks, enqueue `warp:` commands, write `.warp/listener.json` (`agentId` or `pid`, `lastPollAt`, `reason`) with `inbound.py heartbeat`, then shell `sleep` for `pollSeconds`, and repeat. It does not return while the loop is running. It returns on pause or stop, or returns `recycle` when its context is large. A Cursor subagent can still exit. The parent runs `orchestrator.py supervise`. `listener: start` starts exactly one replacement straight away and the restart is logged. `listener: hold` does not start a second. A heartbeat older than `listenerStaleMinutes` is the same start. Herald posts one note, `Listener kept restarting. One listener is running.`, when restarts repeat. `parent-exit` prints `parent: stay` while that listener is supposed to be running, while any ticket is not merged or parked. `parent: exit` is when every ticket is merged or parked, or the run is paused or stopped. Each pass runs supervise, reads `.warp/tickets/<id>/`, patches the live beam, runs `checkout.py launch`, starts one subagent in that worktree, pushes the beam, and passes again. A launch refusal is not idle. Pause and stop still sync state, and then the listener may stop. A running flag from the previous turn does not block the next start. The listener does not implement tickets, does not merge, and does not write product code. The parent runs `inbound.py apply-pending` and patches the live beam. The listener does not start a ticket subagent.
- Close the window, open a new one, `/warp-start`. State comes from main plus ticket folders. Every merge commits the live `.warp/beam.json`, journal, and board onto main. The ticket branch's beam is dropped. The end of each parent tick pushes that same beam. Tokens, cost, API keys, and webhook URLs are stripped. `.warp/config.yaml` is not committed. `/warp-update-state` loads main, patches `.warp/tickets/<id>/` only, and puts back pause, stop, claims, and runState the parent has that main does not. `/warp-pause` and `/warp-stop` run that sync before they return.
- A Shuttle writes `lastSeenAt` with `beam.py heartbeat`. The listener writes `lastSeenAt` and `.warp/listener.json` with `inbound.py heartbeat` on every poll. `beam.py watchdog` runs on every tick and on start and resume. A fresh Shuttle heartbeat is left alone. Pause and stop do not start a replacement. The listener is not replaced by the watchdog. The parent replaces it from `orchestrator.py supervise`. A dead Shuttle becomes `recovering` on the same branch. Past `maxRecoveries` the alarm is `worker-died`. There is no process table. Plugin hooks do not run on cloud runners.
- `/warp-status`, `/warp-start`, and `/warp-resume` list every ticket that is not merged. Alarms and stalls lead, then a count of each state. The same rows are `openWork` on the beam. `orchestrator.py supervise` marks a ticket stalled when its state makes no progress within that state's limit, logs it under `.warp/tickets/<id>/`, and tries one fix per stalled or alarmed ticket up to `maxStallFixes`. Past that cap the ticket parks and Slack gets one alarm. A digest posts every `statusDigestMinutes`. A new stall or alarm posts once.
- The parent tick repairs `lock-escape` only, one ticket at a time, every `alarmRepairMinutes` (default 15). The repair widens that ticket's locks to the paths that escaped. It waits when an in-flight ticket holds one of those paths. Past `maxAlarmRepairs` (default 3) the alarm stays. `bugbot-failed`, `worker-died`, `stuck`, `ci-red`, and `gate-red` are left alone by `alarm_repair.py`. `orchestrator.py supervise` restarts a `worker-died` step, starts a fix Shuttle for `ci-red` and `bugbot-failed`, and restarts `stuck` up to `maxRecoveries`. `gate-red` stays on the gate. Pause and stop do not repair. The listener does not start the repair subagent. The parent starts one subagent in that ticket's worktree.
- A pending or stale-red gate clears when every member is merged or done and no check is actually red, then the tick runs. G1 with members P-002, P-003, P-004, and P-018 stores `members merged: P-002, P-003, P-004, P-018`. Herald posts `G1 pending cleared. Members merged. Tick ran.` A green gate stays green. Pause and stop do not recompute.
- `/warp-upgrade` replaces `.cursor/plugins/warp`. Run it in the repo that has Warp installed. When the source is a git checkout, it fetches the default branch. If the fetch fails, it prints `fetch failed` and `keeping the installed copy`. It does not overwrite `.warp/config.yaml` or the beam. It prints `Warp vX.Y.Z`. Reload Cursor, then confirm with `/warp-version`. `/warp-init` does not upgrade an existing copy. This command does. `--force` is not an upgrade flag. The flags are `--root` and `--source`.
 Init and upgrade write `.cursor/commands/warp-upgrade.md` from `commands/warp-upgrade.md` so a reload lists `/warp-upgrade` when the plugin command index still omits that path.